    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4259",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x+2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246608/assets/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty.\n$$",
